@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# The Daily Grind account UI
+
+A responsive passkey management page in the existing static site. Rename and delete example passkeys, with persistent browser storage, accessible confirmation dialogs, an empty state, and error messages. The default **Demo account** does not modify real credentials.
+
+Run automated checks with `npm test`. No build step is required.
+
+## Connecting a real account
+
+This repository has no authentication service or backend. Set `window.DailyGrindAccount = { apiBase: '/api/account/passkeys', csrfToken: 'server-provided-token' }` before loading `account.js` to use an authenticated API instead of demo data. Serve the account page at `/account` (and optionally `/account/security`) with your host's route mapping. Existing links to other site sections retain their original destinations.
+
+The API contract:
+
+- `GET /api/account/passkeys`: `{ rpId, passkeys: [{ id, name, credentialId, provider, createdAt, lastUsedAt, current, icon }] }`. `credentialId` is the base64url WebAuthn credential ID. `icon` may be `phone` or `laptop`. Dates are ISO dates or timestamps. Return the authenticated customer's credentials only.
+- `PATCH /api/account/passkeys/:id`: JSON `{ name }`; return a successful status after persisting the label. Names are trimmed and limited to 60 characters.
+- `DELETE /api/account/passkeys/:id`: return a successful status only after revoking the credential. Enforce ownership, session authentication, CSRF protection, and any reauthentication or last-credential policies on the server. Non-success responses leave the UI unchanged.
+
+Requests include same-origin session cookies and the configured CSRF header. Do not configure a cross-origin API. Real account identity, rewards, and other site sections are placeholders inherited from the static-site scope and must be populated by the account service.
+
+After successful server deletion, the UI calls `PublicKeyCredential.signalUnknownCredential({ rpId, credentialId })` when available. It does not claim the authenticator actually removed the key: providers decide how to handle signals. Unsupported or failed signals show instructions to remove the entry in the device password manager. A rename changes the account's device label; it is not a change to WebAuthn's user display name and therefore does not call `signalCurrentUserDetails`. Demo credentials are never passed to browser authenticator APIs.
+
+Signal API reference: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/PublicKeyCredential/signalUnknownCredential_static
